// @emit-error only i32[] arrays and struct/union element arrays are supported
// The `null` TYPE spelled ON ITS OWN, rather than as a union member.
//
// Why this file exists. `null` is overwhelmingly written as a union MEMBER
// (`T | null`), and a union member is consumed by the union arm of annotation
// resolution, which recognises it in place — the resolver's standalone `null`
// LEAF never runs for it. A reach probe over the whole standing corpus and over
// 50,400 fuzz programs counted that leaf firing **0** times on either channel:
// the one arm of the annotation resolver with no witness anywhere in the tree.
// D-TSTY wrote this file rather than record the arm as unpinnable.
//
// What it pins is a STAGE, not a message. `null[]` is a type-VALID annotation
// the emitter has no array representation for, so the compile must fail at the
// EMIT stage (rc 3). If the standalone `null` spelling ever stops resolving, the
// element poisons the array, the annotation becomes `unknown type 'null[]'`, and
// the CHECKER rejects at rc 2 — which this directive reports as "failed earlier
// (rc 2 = type)" instead of quietly accepting a different diagnostic. The
// message below is reached only *after* `null` has resolved, which is the point.
//
// The element position is deliberate: it reaches the leaf through the resolver's
// recursion (array-of), not only at an annotation's root.
const xs: null[] = []
print(xs.length)
